            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack, after the ventilation &amp; air sealing work has been addressed &amp; the attic insulation has been upgraded)



** Around the plumbing stack that travels from the basement, up through the adjoining wall between the 2 bathrooms and up into the attic. The opening around this pipe into the attic is obviously not sealed &amp; needs to be. There was also alot of air leakage from below the bathtub (as felt in the basement).



** The older windows in the basement



** The current chimney for the oil furnace &amp; hot water tank as well as the 5" combustion air supply pipe near the basement ceiling. (both of these would no longer be required if you upgrade the furnace and hot water to high efficiency, direct vent units - as outlined elsewhere in this report).



Savings: I estimate you would achieve a minimum 10% air leakage reduction (perhaps 20%). This is estimated to save $ 65 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.9 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate 

(for 10% and maybe $750 rebate if a 20% reduction is achieved) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. Please note that air sealing rebates are only provided as part of the DHERP program.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of this 1968 house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation. It appears the exterior siding of your house is in reasonably good shape so you are not currently planning an exterior siding upgrade. I mentioned that I would still model the impact of adding additional exterior wall insulation.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP)  that was launched on April 1, 2019, can help you cover the cost of additional insulation for exterior walls. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



As the City of Yellowknife requires new housing to have a minimum R30 for exterior walls, I have modeled adding 4" (R20) of extruded polystyrene rigid insulation to the exterior. This insulation would extend down to cover the exterior of the basement header (the 2x10 floor joists between the basement &amp; the main floor) as well.



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to the exterior walls of your house (including the header space), you could potentially save $ 873 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic insulation includes the original fibreglass batt insulation plus some additional blown cellulose on top. There are some high &amp; low spots, and I used an average of R20 total for the attic. I have modeled the opportunity to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R20 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 349 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.2 GJ, and qualify for a $ 750 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. If you do not proceed with a DHERP wall insulation upgrade, the rebate for the ceiling insulation would be $375 through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Savings &amp; rebate for adding R20 to the exterior of the header space is included in the wall insulation upgrade section of this report.</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows on the main floor have been previously upgraded, however there are 2 older single pane wood windows in the basement that are eligible through the DHERP for an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading these 2 older basement windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 75 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates (2 windows = $500 rebate) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house is generally air tight, except for some larger air leaks (outlined elsewhere in this report) that will be eliminated as you complete your renovations and upgrade the furnace &amp; hot water units. There is a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the furnace that brings in outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



If this house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



As you "Renovate Tight", you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right", which means you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 70% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



Savings:

By installing a 70% efficient ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will actually increase the ventilation of this house (which is a good thing) and there is as estimated additional $ 144 per year in energy costs, and an increase to your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ (which is about the same as the savings from air sealing if you achieve a 20% reduction). There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 121 litre oil hot water tank, that is co-vented with the oil furnace up the same chimney. I have modeled an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor (EF) of 0.92 or higher. There are many available in Yellowknife with an EF up to 0.97.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 186 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an oil furnace (estimated 83% efficiency). In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,347 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 31.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not proceed with a DHERP wall insulation upgrade, the rebate for the furnace would be $1,500 through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has 2 older toilets that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify you for a $15,250 rebate from this program, as per the following list.



     - $8,500 for the wall insulation (including the header space)

     - $   500 for the windows

     - $   750 for the attic insulation

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $   500 for the air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate



In addition to the $15,250 rebate from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR instantaneous hot water - $1,250

     - ESTAR ventless (condensing) clothes dryer - $400

     - ECM Pump (for your water circulation pump) - $175

     - Programmable Thermostats - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.3 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace &amp; hot water tank vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. The wood stove has a dedicated combustion air supply connected to the back of it, however this wood stove can still backdraft smoke into the house each time you open the front of the woodstove to add more wood.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present, although not in the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - a kitchen range hood fan'(very little flow &amp; I could not see the vent pipe in the attic)

        - a bathroom fan (very little flow &amp; the vent pipe has a low spot in it that may have filled with water)

        - a clothes dryer (has good air flow - ensure you keep the lint trap &amp; the vent to the outside clean)

        - a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected from the outside to the return air plenum of the furnace

              * these pipes usually have an adjustable damper to increase or decrease the amount of outside air



You will want to have a closer look in the attic to see why the range hood &amp; bathroom fan have very little air flow (blocked or kinked pipes). This is a good time to air seal around the plumbing stack that goes up through the ceiling.



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



If you plan to install an HRV into this house (renovate tight .... ventilate right), than incorporate the bathroom exhaust through the HRV, with a boost switch. Please see the section of my report about a "Ventilation System".



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre delivered

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



